### Constructors: Object Initialization
A constructor is a special member function invoked automatically when an object is created. It shares the exact name of the class and has no return type.
- **Default Constructor**: Takes no arguments.
- **Parameterized Constructor**: Initializes members with custom values.
- **Member Initializer List**: Directly initializes fields before the constructor body executes (vital for efficiency and const members):
```cpp
class Server {
private:
    int port;
public:
    Server(int p) : port(p) { // Initializer list
        std::cout << "Server listening on port " << port << std::endl;
    }
};
```

### Destructors: Automatic Cleanup
A destructor is invoked automatically when an object leaves scope or is deleted (`~ClassName()`).

### The RAII Pattern (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization)
RAII is C++'s most defining architectural pattern:
- Resources (memory, file handles, mutex locks) are acquired in a **constructor**.
- Resources are automatically released in the **destructor**.
- This guarantees zero leaks, even in the presence of runtime exceptions!

