### The Purpose of Inheritance
Inheritance enables a new class (derived/child) to inherit properties and behaviors from an existing class (base/parent), modeling an 'IS-A' relationship (e.g., a `Dog` IS-A `Animal`).

### Syntax of Inheritance
```cpp
class Animal {
public:
    void eat() {
        std::cout << "Eating sustenance" << std::endl;
    }
};

class Dog : public Animal {
public:
    void bark() {
        std::cout << "Woof!" << std::endl;
    }
};
```

### Access Control in Inheritance
- `public` inheritance preserves member visibility: public members of base remain public in derived.
- `protected` members in base become accessible to derived class methods, but remain hidden from external code.

